<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belize City</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a> City is the largest city of Belize, at the mouth of the Belize River on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast. It grew from a settlement of British logwood and mahogany cutters, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baymen">Baymen</a>, and served as capital of British Honduras. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-hattie">Hurricane Hattie</a> devastated it in 1961, a new inland capital was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belmopan">Belmopan</a>, completed in 1970, though Belize City remains the commercial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belize-city.jpg" alt="Belize City" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pgbk1987 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belize_City_Montage.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Belize City stands at the mouth of Haulover Creek, a distributary of the Belize River, which empties into the Caribbean Sea eight kilometres from the city. The city proper is usually divided into two areas: Northside, bounded by Haulover Creek and ending in the east at the Fort George area, and Southside, which includes downtown and the port. Four bridges, among them the Swing Bridge at Market Square, link the divisions, and the main canals are Haulover Creek, Burdon Canal and Collet Canal. The climate is tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, with a long wet season from May through February, and the city is susceptible to flooding in the rainy season.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was founded as Belize Town in 1638 by English lumber harvesters, on the site of a small Maya settlement called Holzuz. The place suited the English as a central post because it was on the sea and was a natural outlet for the local rivers and creeks down which they shipped logwood and mahogany. It also became the home of the thousands of African slaves brought in by the English, and later the British from 1707, to work in the forestry industry.</p><p>Belize Town was the coordination site for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-st-georges-caye">Battle of St. George's Caye</a> in 1798, which the British won against would-be invaders, and the home of the local courts and government officials until the 1970s. For this reason historians often say that the capital was the colony, since the centre of British control lay there. Antonio Soberanis Gómez, George Price and Evan X Hyde all lobbied to take their movements outside the city, but people continued to look to it for guidance.</p><p>Belize City was directly struck by hurricanes in 1931 and 1961, and fires have burnt out stretches of housing at various times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-hattie">Hurricane Hattie</a> swept ashore in October 1961 and almost entirely destroyed the city. The government was moved to the new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belmopan">Belmopan</a>, in 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Belize City is the largest city in Belize, with a population of 63,999 at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and it is the country's principal port and its financial and industrial hub. It has branches of all the major banks and the Central Bank, and the Supreme Court of Belize still sits there.</p><p>St John's Cathedral, begun in 1812 and completed in 1820 of orange bricks that came on British ships as ballast, is the oldest Anglican church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and the only Anglican cathedral outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> where kings have been crowned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belize City” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belmopan, built after Hurricane Hattie, is the capital, though <b>Belize City</b> is the largest town.</blockquote><a href="/state/belize">Belize</a></li><li><blockquote>A hurricane devastated <b>Belize City</b> in 1931.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-honduras">British Honduras</a></li></ul></section>
